---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
import GiveStrip from '../components/GiveStrip.astro';
---
<Base title="Home" color="olive">
  {/* Hero photo: add style="--photo: url('/photos/hero.jpg')" to the section. */}
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Service should not start with debt.</h1>
      <p>Every year, people with the grades and the commitment to lead walk away from a commission over a tuition bill. We give them scholarships, so cost stops deciding who gets to lead.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a href="/how-it-works" class="btn btn-light">How it works</a>
        <a href="/apply" class="btn btn-light">Apply</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <GiveStrip />

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>The best candidates for the military can't afford to be one</h2>
        <p>
          The battlefield isn't a field anymore. It's networks, code, satellites and the systems the country's economy runs on. Leading there takes the kind of education the top programs in engineering, computer science and cyber are built to give.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those programs are also the most expensive. A top university that hosts an ROTC unit costs close to $100,000 a year. ROTC awards are fixed amounts that haven't kept pace with tuition, and the federal loans a family can take out now stop at $65,000, for all four years.
        </p>
        <p>
          So a high school senior with the grades, the drive and the acceptance letter does what thousands of candidates do. She picks a cheaper school than the one that admitted her, takes on the debt, or takes the job offer that pays more. The military never finds out what it lost.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stats">
        <div class="stat">
          <span class="num">58%</span>
          <div class="label">Historically, roughly this share of new officers paid their own way through college (FY2000 data).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <span class="num">$99,139</span>
          <div class="label">USC's cost of attendance for one year (2025–26). Many top universities that host ROTC units cost close to $100,000.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <span class="num">16%</span>
          <div class="label">Share of four years at USC covered by the new $65,000 lifetime cap on federal parent loans, from July 1, 2026.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <span class="num">137%</span>
          <div class="label">Rise in private tuition since Congress last set the ROTC cadet stipend ceiling, in FY2001.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>“We don't lose these candidates in the interview. We lose them at the tuition bill.”</p>
      </blockquote>
      <div class="section-head" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <p>
          That's the gap. The officers we'll ask to lead how this country protects itself are being shaped by what they could afford, not by what they could do. And the ones who make it in carrying debt leave earlier, first from the specialties the country is shortest of: cyber, communications, technology and intelligence.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Who we serve block, in How It Works' color */}
  <section class="block c-gold">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Who we serve</h2>
        <p>
          Our recipients have already raised their hand: a high schooler signing up for ROTC, or an enlisted service member going after the degree they need to commission. They've done the hard part. We make sure money isn't the reason they end up somewhere smaller than their ambition.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's why we don't spread small awards thin. We fund a substantial share of the cost when it changes where a candidate can go, so they can attend the school that admitted them, not the one they could afford.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <Photo hint="A high school student headed to ROTC" ratio="3 / 2" />
          <h3>High school candidates</h3>
          <p class="hook">“You chose to lead. We'll help you get there without the debt.”</p>
          <p>Juniors and seniors headed to a college with ROTC, or in service academy prep.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <Photo hint="An enlisted service member working toward a commission" ratio="3 / 2" />
          <h3>Enlisted service members</h3>
          <p class="hook">“You already answered the call. Now lead.”</p>
          <p>Active-duty enlisted members of any branch pursuing the degree they need to commission, through programs such as Green to Gold, STA-21 or OCS/OTS.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <a href="/how-it-works" class="btn">How it works</a>
        <a href="/apply" class="btn btn-light">Apply</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>About Resolute Ambitions</h2>
        <p>
          Resolute Ambitions supports high-potential students and enlisted service members pursuing the path to military officership. Through scholarships and educational support, we reduce the financial burden that too often stands between talented people and a career of service.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cols">
        <div>
          <ul class="stars">
            <li>
              <h3>The math is broken</h3>
              <p>Most officers start out in debt, the fully funded paths are limited, and early-career pay leaves little room to pay it down.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Chosen to lead</h3>
              <p>Our recipients have already committed: a high schooler signing up for ROTC, or an enlisted service member going after the degree to commission.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Straight to the school</h3>
              <p>A volunteer board, scholarship money paid directly to schools, progress checked before every payment, and an independent selection committee.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Built by people who've lived it</h3>
              <p>A founding circle of officers and professionals who came through the same door.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <Photo hint="Candidates, cadets or a commissioning ceremony" ratio="4 / 5" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Give block, in Give's color */}
  <section class="block block-center c-red">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Paid straight to the school</h2>
      <p>Scholarship money never passes through a recipient's hands. It goes to the school, and we check enrollment and academic progress before every payment.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a href="/give" class="btn">Give</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  {/* Our Story block, in Our Story's color */}
  <section class="block c-navy">
    <div class="container cols">
      <div>
        <h2>Same room, two paths</h2>
        <p>
          Our founders sat in the same unit at 20. One stayed and now develops the officers the country depends on. One left and protected the systems its economy runs on. Both got there because they made it into the room.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="quote quote-left">
          <p>“I got lucky. I would like to be somebody else's luck.”</p>
          <cite>Chris Kennedy, co-founder</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <div class="actions actions-left">
          <a href="/our-story" class="btn">Read our story</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <Photo hint="Portrait of Chris Kennedy" ratio="4 / 5" />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
